opensim-tools: the web side of a grid
=====================================

The scripts the viewers and the simulators query for the economy, the search and the offline messages are the
helpers of this project, in the opensim-helpers package (a dependency of the tools): they are in
/usr/share/opensim-helpers, to be served by a web server. Nothing is enabled by installing the packages. Any other
implementation (w4os, the fork of Kevin Cozens, wiredux) follows the same protocols and can be used instead, nothing
here is needed for it.

Settings
--------

The helpers read the config of the grid as OpenSimulator has it: Robust.ini (the addresses, the message of the day,
the databases), OpenSim.ini and the ini of the simulators (the addresses of the search, the registration and the
offline messages), in /etc/opensim/grids/<grid>/, written by `opensim setup`. Nothing is copied in another file: what
has an official place is read there. Helpers.ini, same format, only holds what has none, in [Helpers] (the source of the
guide, the search database, the theme, the currency...), as a commented template. They are specific to a grid: with
several grids on a machine, the virtual host of each one sets OPENSIM_GRID (the snippets below do).

The helpers serve the addresses the grid gives to its services, those of this site only (the host of WebURL). The
economy address is where they are by default (/helpers, e.g. /helper): currency.php and landtool.php are under it, the
other services are where the grid puts them in its settings. Each query uses the database of its service, as
OpenSimulator chooses it (the ConnectionString of the section of the service, else DatabaseService).

Web server
----------

`opensim web` tells where the services are and what answers;
`opensim setup` writes it for the grid in /etc/opensim/grids/<grid>/web (<grid>.caddyfile, <grid>-nginx.conf, <grid>-apache.conf),
`opensim web <grid> snippet caddy|nginx|apache` writes it again, what the web server needs. Include the result in the
site of the grid; the config of the web server is not touched.

Every ini file of a grid may hold a password: they are readable by their owner and the web server only (mode 640, group
www-data when the machine has one).
